{"approved_definition": "Social status and prestige, control or dominance over people and resources. Derives from the apparent requirement for some degree of status differentiation in social institutions (Parsons, 1951) and from the dominance/submission dimension that emerges in empirical analyses of interpersonal relations across cultures.", "core_value": "power", "sources": [{"entry_id": "9d126412:entry:2", "journal_entry": "Daniel presented my regional consolidation idea at the leadership sync this morning. Same structure, same three-phase approach I pitched to him informally two weeks ago over coffee. He did not credit me. I sat there, mouth half open, and just let it happen.", "nudge_response": null}, {"entry_id": "9d126412:entry:1", "journal_entry": "Managed to restructure the KPI reporting template my department uses before the quarterly review — simplified it, made the data speak for itself instead of burying it in twelve different tabs the way Daniel's team does. My GM noticed during the meeting and called it "clean work." Not a big deal, just a moment where the right thing clicked into place.", "nudge_response": null}, {"entry_id": "9d126412:entry:0", "journal_entry": "Had a good run at the RC meeting tonight. The lobby renovation proposal has been stalling for months because of Uncle Tan and his little coalition of holdouts, but I came prepared this time. Pulled together three contractor quotes, a cost-per-household breakdown, a maintenance projection over ten years. Laid it all out on the whiteboard. Even Mrs. Lim, who has been sitting on the fence, nodded along when I showed the depreciation numbers.

The thing is, nobody else would have done this homework. They would have kept going back and forth at every meeting, complaining about the cracked tiles and the peeling paint, never actually resolving anything. I spent two weekends on those quotes. Called in a favor from a guy I know at a building management firm. That is how things get done — you do the legwork, you control the information, you set the direction.

When the vote came, it was seven to two. Uncle Tan and his friend held out but it did not matter. I kept my voice level, thanked everyone for attending, wrapped up in under ninety minutes. Walked home feeling like myself. Like the version of me that knows how to move a room. It has been a while since I felt that at the office.", "nudge_response": null}], "user_phrase": "Having influence over how things go"}
